1. ONE as a number

Here one simply means 1.

One student was present.

Here, one tells us the number of students.

So:

one = adjective/determiner
student = noun

Another example:

I have one brother.

→ one = number/determiner


2. ONE as a Pronoun

When one replaces a noun, it functions as a pronoun.

Compare:

I don't want the blue pen. I want the red one.

Here one replaces:

pen

So:

one = pronoun

Another example:

I don't want the blue pens. I want the red ones.

Here ones replaces:

pens

Therefore:

one → replaces a singular countable noun
ones → replaces a plural countable noun


3. ONE = “a person / people in general”

This is another very important use.

One should help others.

Here one doesn't mean the number 1.

It means:

a person / anybody / everyone in general

So one = indefinite pronoun.

Another example:

One should respect one's elders.

Meaning:

A person should respect their elders.


4. ONE → ONE'S → ONESELF

This is one of the most important parts of Rule 37.

When one means a person/everybody in general, maintain the same pronoun system.

Subject:

One should do one's duties.

Possessive:

one's duties

Reflexive:

oneself

So:

One should do one's duties oneself. ✅

Not:

❌ One should do their duties themselves.

For the traditional grammar rule used in competitive exams, keep the pronouns consistent with one.

Pattern:

ONE → ONE'S → ONESELF

Memorize this as a set.



“One of the students forgot to bring his/her/their/one's book.”

This is an important exam question.

One of the students forgot to bring his book. ✅

Traditional competitive-exam grammar treats one here as referring to one student, so the pronoun should agree with that singular person.

The notes give his as the answer.

